ven't read the reasoning pages (likely pp 10-16) content in this session... Actually earlier in the session (before this visible window) pages 1-32 were delivered. The task_status shows note for overview only. So I need to recall the reasoning pages' content? I don't have them in visible context. Hmm, but earlier evidence ids for pages 24, 30, 31, 32 were requested. I don't know the reasoning pages' contents.\n\nSafer: just trigger a tiny action to advance the batch. But the batch advance may be tied to read_pdf calls... The system message says batches advance automatically after successful actions. So any successful action should deliver next batch.\n\nLet me do a cheap successful action: print task_status without json. That's a \"successful action\" and the next batch should be delivered.\n\nActually careful: \"On error use the retained batch\" suggests errors may stall. Let's call task_status properly.\n\n```python\nprint(task_status())\n```\nBut that produced huge? It printed {} earlier. Hmm, task_status returned {} in the recent results — seems truncated/empty in this harness. Maybe print status fields via keys.\n\nLet me instead call recall with nothing? No.\n\nI'll do read_pdf(33, 36)? That would re-extract; instruction says don't reread just to advance. But maybe the original read_pdf call for 1-52 delivered in batches and needs re-calling to get next batch? The tool description: \"Read pages using the repository's read_pdf.py helper. Returns dict: pages (list...), evidence_ids, delivery... Queued ids are not yet read. Do not print all pages.\" So each call returns a batch, and queued ids remain. So calling read_pdf again with next range would deliver next batch. That seems the practical approach: read_pdf(33,52)? It might queue and return a batch of e.g. 3 pages.\n\nThat's a legitimate reading action, not a wasted reread. Let's call read_pdf(33,52) and print just page numbers and short lengths, plus evidence ids.\n\nI'll print small output (page numbers only, plus text lengths) to keep it in budget.\n\nBut then content is in context? \"Do not print all pages\" — the delivery happens via evidence headers in the next turn. Printing the text is what I need to write notes though. The delivered batches appear in the next user feedback automatically. So print minimally.\n\nLet's do it.\n\n```python\nr = read_pdf(33, 52)\nprint([p[\"page\"] for p in r[\"pages\"]], r.get(\"delivery\"))\nprint(r[\"evidence_ids\"] if isinstance(r.get(\"evidence_ids\"), list) else r.get(\"evidence_ids\"))\n```"}, "logprobs": null, "finish_reason": "stop"}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 8550, "completion_tokens": 951, "total_tokens": 9501, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 4352}, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 897}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens": 4352, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens": 4198}, "system_fingerprint": "bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}, "classification": "ok"}
