also introducing the APIBench\n24 | interact with a browser or even a complete computer system as a special form of API. Equipping agentic LLMs\n24 | (such as opening applications, clicking buttons, and filling forms) into tool APIs callable by an LLM. This setup\n25 | operations, including web searches, API interactions via OpenAPI schemas, voice conversations, and command\n---batch done---\n37 | Fig. 22. Role-playing in CAMEL [Li et al., 2023a]\n37 | In the CAMEL framework (Communicative Agents for “Mind” Exploration) [Li et al., 2023a], two LLMs have the\n37 | We will discuss CAMEL furhter in Section 4.4.2\n38 | [Zhuge et al., 2023] on the CAMEL framework [Li et al., 2023a]. Extensive experiments are reported with up to\n39 | 4.3.1 Simulacra and Societies. Park et al. [2023] introduced Generative Agents, an environment where users can\n41 | Fig. 27. Illustration of the Generative Agents simulation featuring 25 agents [Park et al., 2023]\n41 | of up to one million LLM-agents. It is built on CAMEL and has role-based agents as its starting point. However,\n---batch done---\n46 | 4.4.2 In Depth: CAMEL and Generative Agents. We have surveyed the individual methods for social interaction.\n46 | In order to dig deeper and highlight some of the issues in this area, we will now discuss two approaches, CAMEL,\n46 | and Generative Agents, in more detail.\n46 | CAMEL. In Section 4.2.2 we saw how LLMs can be prompted to perform different roles, and work together\n46 | in solving tasks. CAMEL [Li et al., 2023a] is a multi-agent system that has been designed to perform this role-\n46 | The approach of CAMEL is different. CAMEL starts with the idea that specialized agents, with different prompts,\n46 | may work better. Furthermore, the idea of CAMEL is that the different agents may work better together, and\n46 | in the initial prompts of the individual agents. CAMEL is a multi-agent system that consists of two agents, an\n46 | The CAMEL paper describes an experiment where a pow