e-scale open-ended emergent behavior at society level.\n45 | between LLM agents while improving task performance. Role-playing frameworks, AI feedback loops, and debate\n46 | 4.4.2 In Depth: CAMEL and Generative Agents. We have surveyed the individual methods for social interaction.\n46 | In order to dig deeper and highlight some of the issues in this area, we will now discuss two approaches, CAMEL,\n46 | and Generative Agents, in more detail.\n46 | CAMEL. In Section 4.2.2 we saw how LLMs can be prompted to perform different roles, and work together\n46 | in solving tasks. CAMEL [Li et al., 2023a] is a multi-agent system that has been designed to perform this role-\n46 | The approach of CAMEL is different. CAMEL starts with the idea that specialized agents, with different prompts,\n46 | may work better. Furthermore, the idea of CAMEL is that the different agents may work better together, and\n46 | in the initial prompts of the individual agents. CAMEL is a multi-agent system that consists of two agents, an\n46 | write a program to solve the problem that the user agent specifies. Both agents are given a so-called inception\n46 | The CAMEL paper describes an experiment where a powerful LLM, GPT4, generates the initial prompt, and a\n46 | In such a setup where agents work with agents, the training process may become unstable. CAMEL reports\n46 | performance on the HumanEval benchmark improving form 30% to around 50%. The CAMEL experiments showed\n46 | Generative Agents. The architecture of CAMEL recognized two types of agents, assistant and user. The next\n46 | from 10-1000. The first paper [Park et al., 2023] introduces the generative agents’ architecture, an architecture\n46 | than in CAMEL, arond 5-15, and the agent architecture is also more involved. Where in CAMEL the behavior was\n46 | specified fully in-context, in the prompts that are exchanged, in Generative Agents there are additional external\n47 | the longer-term of the agent’s behavior. The LLM that is used is GPT-3.5, the same as in CAMEL.\n47 | Whether the Generative Agents system generates believable behavior is evaluated with the help of 100 human\n47 | evaluators. These were enlisted to rank believability of the communication patterns on four categories, by\n47 | that generative agents remember with embellishments, and that reflection is required for synthesis of memories.\n47 | behavior in artificial conditions, in what-if scenarios. When do we benefit from more competition, when from\n47 | and simulation studies will provide insight into social science questions. In particular, topics of interest are the\n47 | influence of LLMs on democratic processes and cyber security, role playing, society of minds, theory of mind,\n48 | 5      General Discussion and Research Agenda\n48 | previous two categories: social behavior benefits from reasoning and self reflection, social actions are increasingly\n48 | 5.1        Research Agenda for Agentic LLM\n48 | Our survey has yielded interesting directions for a research agenda for agentic LLMs, which we will now discuss\n48 | in more detail. Please refer to Table 4 for a summary of the agenda.\n48 | Training Data. The benefit from language corpuses that are used for pretraining of LLMs is said to be plateauing.\n49 | first item for the agenda for further research. It is interesting to see how the reinforcement learning methods that\n50 | features prominently on the research agenda for agentic LLMs, with mechanistic interpretability and open world\n50 | generative agents [Park et al., 2024]. Some behavior patterns in multi-agent simulations only emerge at scale,\n50 | Knowledge distillation in LLM agents in an important item for our research agenda.\n50 | et al., 2024]? Self reflection by LLMs is another item for the research agenda.\n51 | Topic               Challenge\n51 | Agent Behavior      Scalable simulation infrastructure, role playing\n51 | Table 4. Summary of Research Agenda for Agentic LLM\n51 | The question if LLMs with outside memory exhibit a personality is a topic for future research.\n51 | of agentic LLMs can be enjoyed. Safety and ethics will become important topics on the research agenda of agentic\n52 | 5.2        Conclusion\n52 | and all categories benefit from reasoning and self-verification. Closing the cycle, the acting and interacting\nLast output from code snippet:\n", "observations_images": null, "action_output": "", "token_usage": {"input_tokens": 8989, "output_tokens": 2312, "total_tokens": 11301}, "is_final_answer": false}}
